WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:04.840
To celebrate the release of Tomorrowland,
let's take a look at the many Easter Eggs

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.630
and references in Disney's live-action adventure
movie.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:14.230
Writer and director Brad Bird may have turned
down the opportunity to direct Star Wars Episode

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:18.179
VII because it clashed with his schedule for
making Tomorrowland, but he still managed

00:00:18.179 --> 00:00:21.120
to include several Star Wars easter eggs in
his movie.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.940
As Star Wars fans know, those movies have
a little thing for chopping characters arms

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:25.940
or hands off!

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:30.890
So, in a nod to Star Wars, Tomorrowland shows
George Clooney's character, Frank, chopping

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.930
off the hand of one of the baddies who's attacking
Britt Robertson's character Casey in Frank's

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:35.930
home.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:41.010
And there are tons of other Star Wars nods
in the movie too in the Blast From The Past

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.010
store.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:45.600
When Keegan-Michael Key's character, Hugo,
makes his dramatic entrance, you can hear

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.160
John Williams' Star Wars theme!

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.719
There's a life-size replica of Han Solo in
carbonite from The Empire Strikes Back.

00:00:51.719 --> 00:00:54.109
There's a poster of Yoda on the wall.

00:00:54.109 --> 00:00:57.789
And there's also a Yoda head on a shelf by
the life-size Stormtrooper.

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:01.829
There's also what looks like an All-Terrain
Scout Transport on the shelf.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:03.859
And there's an R2-D2.

00:01:03.859 --> 00:01:08.410
And did you notice the Millennium Falcon,
the various TIE fighters hanging from the

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.200
ceiling, and what looks like a Death Star?

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.979
And check out that photo of C3PO &amp; R2-D2.

00:01:14.979 --> 00:01:19.750
And let's not forget the bunch of light sabers
on the wall, the Stormtrooper helmet, &amp; Darth

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.670
Vader helmet on the shelf, and the illuminated
sign that says Star Wars: The Empire Strikes

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:25.670
Back.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:26.670
Plus there's a brass lightsaber.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.600
And there's a Stormtrooper figure on the shelf.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.930
And then of course there's all those Star
Wars comic books.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:36.860
And check out Kathryn Hahn's hair in the movie!

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:41.950
As Ursula Gernsback, she sports Princess Leia-style
buns on the side of her head!

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:48.000
When the movie's art department designed the
1984 version of the jet pack we see in Tomorrowland,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.560
they were influenced by Star Wars, particularly
in its shiny, white metallic finish, which

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:53.950
was inspired by Storm Troopers.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:59.439
Of course, there's a wealth of other Disney
easter eggs and references too.

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:04.509
There's a photo of Walt Disney, who created
the Tomorrowland section of Disneyland in

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:07.070
1955.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:10.990
And if you look carefully there are a few
Mickey Mouses hidden about the Blast From

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:13.720
The Past store.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:19.010
Plus there's an album cover from the soundtrack
of Disney's sci-fi movie from 1979, The Black

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.010
Hole!

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:26.350
When Tomorrowland opened it had on show the
sets of the Nautilus from Disney's live-action

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:31.770
sci-fi movie 20,000 Leagues Under The Sea,
which was released in 1954 and was based on

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.320
Jules Verne's 19th-century novel of the same
name.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.320
Which is interesting because the look of the
rocket ship in the movie Tomorrowland was

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:36.320
inspired by the design of the Nautilus submarine
from the movie 20,000 Leagues.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.630
And, if you look carefully, you can see a
1965 vinyl record of “Jonny Quest in 20,000

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:44.270
Leagues Under The Sea” in the Blast From
The Past store.

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:48.810
And there's lots of Jonny Quest collectibles
including a Questworld Cyber Copter.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:53.690
Jonny Quest was an animated sci-fi series
from Hanna-Barbera that started in the mid-60s

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:58.390
and followed a boy's adventures with his scientist
father.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:03.220
During the World's Fair in the movie, you
can spot The White Rabbit from Disney's 1951

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:08.140
film Alice In Wonderland and the Big Bad Wolf
from Disney's short film Three Little Pigs,

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:11.990
which won the Oscar for Best Animated Short
Film in 1934.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:18.150
Among the stuff Casey gets back from the local
police is a pack of Beeman's gum, which is

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:23.570
a nod to The Right Stuff, an early 80s movie
about military test pilots in America's first

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.560
efforts at manned spaceflight.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.270
In The Right Stuff, pilot Chuck Yeager asks
for some Beeman's gum before a flight.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:35.751
The same gum also saved the day in the plot
of 1991 Disney movie The Rocketeer, which

00:03:35.751 --> 00:03:42.390
brought to life the jet-packed superhero created
by comic book artist Dave Stevens.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:47.450
When Casey tells Frank that she knows how
gadgets and things work, Frank replies, ‘Well,

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:48.450
zip-a-dee-doo for you.'

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.550
That's a reference to the song Zip-A-Dee-Doo-Dah
which featured in Disney's live-action and

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:59.450
animated film Song Of The South and won the
Oscar for Best Original Song in 1947.

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:04.650
Tomorrowland features Disneyland's It's A
Small World ride, which, in the movie, acts

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:08.920
as a portal that takes a young Frank to the
futuristic city of Tomorrowland.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.870
The ride was created especially for the 1964
World's Fair in New York and is made up of

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:17.000
animatronic dolls representing the children
of the world.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.370
The ride's accompanying song It's A Small
World (After All) was written by Robert and

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:26.510
Richard Sherman, who wrote many movie scores
including Mary Poppins and Chitty Chitty Bang

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:27.510
Bang.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:31.540
The song's optimistic theme of world peace
and international unity was a response to

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.220
the Cuban Missile Crisis.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:37.180
In the movie, as well as It's A Small World
(After All), you can also hear the brothers'

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:41.630
song There's A Great Big Beautiful Tomorrow,
which they wrote for Disney's Carousel of

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:46.470
Progress attraction at the 1964 World's Fair!

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:51.350
As well as writing the score for Tomorrowland,
composer Michael Giacchino also makes a cameo

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:57.170
appearance as the operator of the It's A Small
Word ride at the 1964 World Fair.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:03.070
Giacchino also composed the music for Tomorrowland
director Brad Bird's movies Ratatouille, The

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:06.710
Incredibles and Mission: Impossible – Ghost
Protocol.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:11.340
It's interesting that it's a pin that takes
Casey to the city of Tomorrowland as Disney's

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.900
theme parks have long sold collectible pins,
and the tradition of Disney Pin Trading has

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.900
been around since Disney World's Millennium
Celebration in late 1999.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:25.850
For the Tomorrowland pin, the movie's design
team used the orange and blue colours of the

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:27.000
1964 World's Fair.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.740
According to director Brad Bird, they wanted
something that felt a bit retro but also classic,

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:36.400
and they used the universal symbol of the
atom like a rising sun in the background.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:42.020
As for the letter T, they tweaked it a bit
to make it look like a jet pack that's blasting

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:43.170
off.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:48.520
Among the buildings we see on the skyline
of the city of Tomorrowland is Space Mountain,

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:53.900
the popular ride that opened in 1975 in the
Tomorrowland section of the Magic Kingdom

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:56.970
theme park in Florida's Disney World.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:02.000
There's also a bunch of references to the
work of Tomorrowland director Brad Bird and

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.000
Pixar.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.100
So, there's a Bart Simpson doll hanging from
the shelf in the Blast From The Past store.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:12.120
Bird was executive consultant on The Simpsons
for many years and directed several episodes

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.600
as well as the music video Do The Bartman.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:18.670
Which is interesting because the Bart doll
in Tomorrowland is wearing the purple mask

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:20.900
of his alter-ego, The Bartman!

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:25.350
Other Simpsons easter eggs include:
a plush toy of the show's comic book superhero

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.690
Radioactive Man, whose catchphrase is ‘Up
and atom!'

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:33.260
and a figure of The Collector, who's the villainous
alter-ego of Comic Book Guy and appeared in

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:36.270
Season 11's Treehouse of Horror X.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:42.070
Brad Bird directed an animated feature film
called The Iron Giant, which is about a young

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:44.570
boy and a robot in the 1950s.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:49.020
And there's a whole host of The Iron Giant
collectibles, including figures and artwork,

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:50.900
in the Blast From The Past store.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:56.960
Rock ‘Em Sock ‘Em Robots is a boxing robots
game created in the mid-60s.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:01.560
It features a Red Rocker and a Blue Bomber
and the idea is for the players to knock the

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:03.170
other robot's head loose!

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:08.580
The Rock ‘Em Sock ‘Em robots have a speaking
cameo in Pixar's Toy Story 2, and can also

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:15.020
be seen among Bob Parr, aka Mr Incredible's
things in The Incredibles, which was written

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:18.020
and directed by Tomorrowland director Brad
Bird!

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:23.450
And there's loads of other pop culture goodies
around the Blast From The Past store too.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:24.450
And scattered throughout the store there's
more geeky goodies.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:28.450
Keegan-Michael Key and Kathryn Hahn play Hugo
and Ursula Gernsback.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:33.639
The name of Key's character is a nod to publisher
Hugo Gernsback who launched Amazing Stories

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:37.920
in 1926, which was the first magazine devoted
to science fiction.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:43.480
There was actually a 1920s copy of Amazing
Stories in the box labelled 1952 which was

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:48.030
discovered in the Disney archive and sparked
the idea for the movie Tomorrowland.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:53.130
Interestingly enough, Brad Bird's movie The
Incredibles featured in a much later edition

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:54.500
of Amazing Stories!

00:07:54.500 --> 00:08:01.550
There's a lot of Marvin the Martian collectibles,
including toys, a snow globe, folder, and

00:08:01.550 --> 00:08:03.130
cups on the counter and the shelves.

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:07.930
And from The Day The Earth Stood Still there's
a life-size version of the robot Gort.

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:14.050
Toy Story 2's Darth Vader-esque evil emperor
Zurg, who's Buzz Lightyear's arch-enemy, also

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:15.990
puts in an appearance in the store.

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:22.550
Plus there's a Strobot Robot 3000, a 1980s
walking robot with flashing red eyes and laser.

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:27.880
And there's a Chief Robotman, a popular Japanese
robot from Yoshiya in the 1960s.

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:33.490
Another robot, this time from the 50s, is
the wind-up Space Robot, Sparky, who walks

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:34.490
and sparks from his mouth!

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:36.419
Plus there's the Lilliput Robot, which is
considered the first tin toy robot made in

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:37.419
late 1930s Japan.

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:41.200
There's a nod to Marvel with the Spider-Man
box that you can make out behind the Chief

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:50.880
Robotman toy in front of it.

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:56.080
Also on the counter we see Omnibot a small
personal robot made in the 1980s, that had

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.120
a cassette player, digital clock, the ability
to carry objects including a tray, and much

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:02.120
more!

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:06.380
Thanks to a T-shirt in the shop there's a
nod to Flash Gordon, who was the hero of a

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:12.900
sci-fi comic strip created by artist Alex
Raymond in the 1930s, and the star of a movie

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:18.620
serial, various live-action and animated TV
series, and, of course, the cult 1980 movie

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:19.620
Flash Gordon.

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:25.140
There's a poster for the sci-fi horror movie
Dr Cyclops, about a mad scientist in the Amazon

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.430
Jungle who shrinks his colleagues.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:31.860
The film was directed by Ernest B. Schoedsack
and released in 1940.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:37.040
Schoedsack also co-directed, with Merian C
Cooper, the 1933 movie King Kong, which you

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:39.460
can see a figure of on the counter.

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:43.810
Now what other easter eggs and references
did you spot in Tomorrowland?

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:45.730
Let me know in the comments below!

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:50.080
If you like this video, please do share it,
hit the thumbs-up button &amp; subscribe for my

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.380
movie reviews and interviews!

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:54.850
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:56.190
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

